summary:
Agents pick MCP tools by reading name , description and inputSchema from tools/list . If those are vague, the model picks the wrong tool, or skips the right one. Glama recently published an open spec for scoring this (TDQS, https://tdqs.dev ), and the main complaint it addresses is inconsistent tool definitions. Before you submit a server to a directory, read what an agent actually sees. Here is a dependency-free Node script that spawns any stdio MCP server, runs the handshake, and prints every tool. // dump-tools.mjs - usage: node dump-tools.mjs <command> [args...] import { spawn } from " node:child_process " ; const [ cmd , ... args ] = process . argv . slice ( 2 ); const p = spawn ( cmd , args , { stdio : [ " pipe " , " pipe " , " inherit " ] }); let buf = "" ; const pending = new Map (); let id = 0 ; p . stdout . on ( " data " , ( d ) => { buf += d ; let i ; while (( i = buf . indexOf ( " 
 " )) >= 0 ) { const line = buf . slice ( 0 , i ). trim (); buf = buf . slice ( i + 1 ); if ( ! line ) continue ; try { const m = JSON . parse ( line ); if ( m . id != null && pending . has ( m . id )) pending . get ( m . id )( m ); } catch {} } }); const send = ( method , params ) => new Promise (( res ) => { const myId = ++ id ; pending . set ( myId , res ); p . stdin . write ( JSON . stringify ({ jsonrpc : " 2.0 " , id : myId , method , params }) + " 
 " ); }); await send ( " initialize " , { protocolVersion : " 2024-11-05 " , capabilities : {}, clientInfo : { name : " dump-tools " , version : " 0.1.0 " }, }); p . stdin . write ( JSON . stringify ({ jsonrpc : " 2.0 " , method : " notifications/initialized " }) + " 
 " ); const { result } = await send ( " tools/list " , {}); for ( const t of result . tools ) { const props = Object . keys ( t . inputSchema ?. properties ?? {}); console . log ( `## ${ t . name } 
 ${ t . description ?? " (NO DESCRIPTION) " } 
params: ${ props . join ( " , " ) || " none " } 
` ); } p . kill (); What to look for in the output Missing descriptions. Any (NO DESCRIPTION) line is a tool the model has to guess about. Descriptions that only restate the name. get_user: Gets a user tells the model nothing. Say what it returns and when to use it instead of a sibling tool. Undescribed parameters. Check each property in inputSchema for its own description , units and an example. Overlapping tools. If two descriptions could apply to the same request, add "use this when... not for..." wording. Making it a deliverable A terminal dump is fine for you, but reviewers and users want a readable page. I packaged the same idea as a small zero-dependency CLI called MCP Tool Docs Generator. It connects to a stdio MCP server, calls initialize and tools/list , and writes a TOOLS.md , one Markdown page per tool, and a tools.json . It is pay-what-you-want with a $1 minimum: https://renevibe76.gumroad.com/l/bmzwpm The script above is free to copy and enough to audit your own server. The packaged version is for when you want the files generated for your README.
